    id: "les-ondes-courtes",
    title: "Les Ondes Courtes",
    genre: "French electro-chanson",
    description: "Hypnagogic night-drive lullaby for the static altar sequence.",
    episode: "Episode 03",
    stylePrompt: "French Cabaret, Nu-Jazz, Electro-Chanson, hypnagogic night drive, sleepy/half-conscious, 75 BPM lullaby tempo, brushed snare with mallet soft hits, breathy hi-hat with loose tension, shimmering vibraphone with long tremolo, upright bass with slow bowed long tones (not walking), accordion with slow bellows exhaustion, Wurlitzer electric piano through tube amp with sagging bias, tape saturation and wow/flutter, AM radio bandwidth restriction (limited 5kHz frequency range), female chanteuse vocals with ribbon mic proximity effect and heavy plate reverb, close whispered delivery drifting into spoken word, auto-panning at 0.5Hz (slow oceanic sway), sidechain compression pumping like sleepy breathing, urban night atmosphere with distant siren doppler shifts, dashboard clock tick as percussion, tape hiss and vinyl crackle as textural bed, hypnagogic jerk sound design (sudden micro-arpeggios), unstable pitch drifting analog warmth, fading-into-silence dynamic contour.",
    lyrics: "[Verse 1]\nThe dashboard glow is the only sun\nA greenish hum that says the night is done\nBut I’m still rolling on the périphérique\nCounting the reflectors, monotonous and chic\nYour voice on the radio is AM thin\nCrackling with the static leaking in\nThe windshield wipers keep a sleepy time\nBrushing the visions that aren’t quite mine\n[Pre-Chorus]\nLes paupières lourdes, le volant froid\nThe road is rocking me like a cradle\nEvery yellow line is a lullaby\nThe bass is slow and the treble’s unstable\n[Chorus]\nLes ondes courtes, dans le noir\nCarrying my half-conscious heart\nOn drift et on rêve, entre deux villes\nThe signal fades and the silence fills\nYour accordion breathes so slow\nLike the tide coming in below\nLes ondes courtes, 75 and low\nI’m letting go\n[Verse 2]\nThe streetlamps stretch into a mercury blur\nEach one a note that’s barely sure\nI pass the same café three times or four\nThe clock on the tower says 3:44\nThe upright bass is walking underwater\nHalf-time feel, getting slower and slower\nA vibraphone is trembling in the back\nLike the rattle of a coffee can, black and slack\n[Pre-Chorus]\nUne static, une chanson, un soupir\nThe FM needle drifts to the left\nThe tape hiss whispers dormir, dormir\nAnd I’m awake but only by theft\n[Chorus]\nLes ondes courtes, dans le noir\nCarrying my half-conscious heart\nOn drift et on rêve, entre deux villes\nThe signal fades and the silence fills\nYour accordion breathes so slow\nLike the tide coming in below\nLes ondes courtes, 75 and low\nI’m letting go\n[Bridge – Spoken/Whispered, barely audible]\nGauche... droite... les lignes ondulent...\nUne station étrangère... Suisse? Belgique?\nLe bruit de la cassette qui se termine...\nClick.\nLe moteur fait mmm... mmm... mmm...\nJe vois des moutons dans les nuages de diesel\nEt ta voix devient une clarinette\nSolo sur un lit de nappes en papier\n[Verse 3]\nThe exit sign is a watercolor stain\nI think I missed my turn again\nBut it doesn’t matter in this heavy haze\nWhere the jazz is soft and the night obeys\nThe rhythm of the tires on the tar\nIs a brushed snare that’s spread too far\nYou sing something about la lune et toi\nBut the words dissolve into the dashboard’s joy\n[Verse 4 – Barely there, fading]\nNow the piano is a music box\nWinding down, losing the locks\nThe strings are rubber bands getting soft\nThe city is a cough that’s way up north\nI should pull over, find a place to sleep\nBut the groove is gentle and the canyon’s deep\nSo I let the wheel guide itself\nAnd become the music, become the shelf\nWhere the radio rests its weary head\nBonsoir... bonsoir...\n[Final Chorus – Ghostly, reverbed]\nLes ondes courtes... dans le noir...\nCarrying my... half-conscious... heart...\nOn drift... et on rêve...\nThe signal... fades...\n[Outro – 30 seconds of decay]\n(Tape slowing down, pitch dropping)\nLa route... en... stéréo...\n(Vinyl crackle and parking brake creak)\nShhhhhhh...\n(Silence, then a single far-off trumpet note)",
